# Adjacent Skills vs Invented Experience

When tailoring the resume to a job posting, make it as compelling as possible. Add skills the user can pick up quickly because they already have a close equivalent. Do not invent jobs, products, dates, metrics, or work that never happened.

## Allowed: related skills and tooling

Put adjacent tools in `skills` (and in the summary if the JD is stacked around them) whenever they help ATS match and are a fair stretch from the real stack.

OK stretches (user-confirmed examples):

- SQL when the background includes Postgres and MongoDB
- Tailwind CSS when the background includes Bootstrap, Chakra, and Sass
- Angular when the background includes React and Vue
- Same-category CSS libraries, UI kits, ORMs, query languages, or cloud services

Prefer the Skills section for stretched tools. Do not write experience bullets that claim you shipped production work in a tool you have never used on that job.

## Not allowed

- Fake employers, titles, dates, products, or features
- Invented metrics or outcomes
- Unrelated stacks with no transfer (e.g. native iOS or Java/Spring from a JS full-stack background)
- Calling a stretched skill "expert" or "years of production X" when it was never used in production
